# Steps

A routine's `steps` run in order, one to 10 of them. Each step is an object holding exactly one kind of step's key, with its settings, and maybe `onlyIf`: `{"snooze": {"days": 3}}`, `{"pin": {}}`.

## The kinds of step

- `notify` (Notify me): A banner and an entry in the Notifications page, with the reason.
  - `note`: a line of the user's own the notice carries beside the reason, at most 200 characters.
  - `banner`: `true` for a banner, `false` for an entry in the Notifications page alone; left out, the routine's notices decide.
- `pin`: Pins it in the inbox, marked with the routine that did it. It takes no settings: `{}`.
- `snooze`: Snoozes it for a number of days, under your wake rules. What you set aside yourself stays as you left it.
  - `days`: Days to snooze, a whole number, 1 to 365 days, default 3.
- `dismiss`: Dismisses it from the inbox until it's restored. What you set aside yourself stays as you left it. It takes no settings: `{}`.
- `restore`: Brings it back from a snooze or a dismissal, yours too, to your attention when the routine's conditions hold. It takes no settings: `{}`.
- `markDone` (Mark the GitHub notification done): Marks this item's GitHub notifications done, as the Notifications page's Done does. It changes only your own notifications, and one marked done can be moved back on GitHub. It takes no settings: `{}`.
- `github` (GitHub actions): The app's own writes, as the review and issue sheets send them, each pinned to the head read right before it goes. A list of actions: topic `github`.
- `agent` (Run a coding agent): Starts the routine's agent on the item with a library prompt. Topic `agent`.
- `script` (Run one of my scripts): Runs one of your own Repo scripts in the item's clone, in the app, as Repo scripts runs it. A script set to ask before running waits for your OK each time.
  - `id`, needed: the script's id, exactly as `autopilot_my_setup` lists it.
  - `name`, needed: the script's name, exactly as `autopilot_my_setup` lists it.
- `waitForChecks` (Wait for the checks): Follows the checks on the agent's pushed commit until every one has finished and settled. Pull requests only.
  - `settleMinutes`: Minutes to settle, a whole number, 0 to 60 minutes, default 2.
  - `giveUpHours`: Hours before giving up, a whole number, 1 to 24 hours, default 2.
- `merge` (Merge it): Once the checks passed, turns auto-merge back on if it was on, else merges when GitHub allows. Pull requests only.
  - `how`, needed: `autoMergeIfItWasOn` (auto-merge if it was on, else when GitHub allows) or `whenMergeable` (when GitHub allows).
  - `method`: `merge` (merge commit), `squash`, or `rebase`; left out, the repository's own method.
  - `allowBypass`: `true` lets a `merge` with no agent step, in a routine whose trigger is `checks_passed`, merge the user's own pull request past the rules when an approval from someone else is all it lacks, every commit signed by them, with `how` `whenMergeable` alone; it merges so only once the user ticks it as they add the routine and turns on the switch in Autopilot's settings, both theirs alone; propose it only when they ask to merge past the rules or without a reviewer; left out, `false`, and it merges only what GitHub allows.
  - `allowSensitive`: `true`, with `allowBypass`, lets it do so when the pull request changes workflow, agent, or tooling files too; propose it only when the user asks for those files to merge so too; left out, `false`.
- `postReview` (Post the agent's review): Posts what a read-only agent step wrote, as a comment or as its verdict. Pull requests only.
  - `as`, needed: `comment` (as a comment) or `verdict` (as its verdict).
  - `allowApprove`: `true` lets a verdict approve, with `as` `verdict` alone; left out, `false`, and an approval posts as a comment.
- `tellMe` (Tell me): A notice when the routine reaches this step, with what happened.
  - `when`: `reached` (that it got here) or `verdict` (how the steps before it ended); left out, `reached`.

## How the modes run them

- `notify`, `pin`, `snooze`, `dismiss`, `restore`, `markDone`, and `tellMe` run at once, wherever they sit, needing no OK: they change only what this Mac shows and tells, and the user's own GitHub notifications.
- `github`, `agent`, `script`, `merge`, and `postReview` follow the routine's mode: in Ask first, the first of them waits for the user's OK, and the rest go on their own; on Autopilot, they run at once. `merge` and `postReview` ask again in Ask first once they are reached.
- `waitForChecks` follows the agent's push: it waits for the checks on the agent's own commit.
- In Dry run nothing runs: the routine records what it would have done.

## Only if

`onlyIf` runs a step only when an earlier step ended so. A step whose `onlyIf` doesn't hold is skipped, and the routine goes on, so "if the fix passes, merge; if it still fails, tell me" is two steps, `{"merge": {"how": "whenMergeable"}, "onlyIf": "passed"}` and `{"tellMe": {}, "onlyIf": "stillFails"}`. An unknown or overtaken verdict holds neither. The outcomes:

- `passed` (the checks passed): from the latest `waitForChecks` step before it.
- `stillFails` (the checks still fail): from the latest `waitForChecks` step before it.
- `pushed` (it pushed): from the latest `agent` step before it.
- `notPushed` (it didn't push): from the latest `agent` step before it.
- `approve` (its verdict was approve): from the latest `agent` step before it.
- `requestChanges` (its verdict was request changes): from the latest `agent` step before it.

## The order rules

`autopilot_check` holds the steps to these, as the builder does:

- Each step holds exactly one kind of step.
- One `agent` step at most: two agents on one item take two routines.
- `waitForChecks` only after an `agent` step in the background (`where` `background`) whose tools preset may push (`editTestPush` or `profile`), and never with `onlyIf` `notPushed`.
- `merge` only after a `waitForChecks` whose `onlyIf` is `pushed`, so the checks it follows are on the agent's own commit, and only with its own `onlyIf` `passed`; or, in a routine with no `agent` step and no `waitForChecks`, whose trigger is `checks_passed` and whose `limits.oncePer` is left out or `headCommit`, with no `onlyIf`, and with no `rebase` or `updateBranch` action and no `script` step before it: it merges the commit the checks passed on.
- `allowBypass` and `allowSensitive` only on a `merge` with no `agent` step, as above, with `how` `whenMergeable`; `allowSensitive` only with `allowBypass`.
- `postReview` only after an `agent` step in the background whose tools preset is `readOnly`, and `allowApprove` only with `as` `verdict`.
- After an `agent` step in the terminal (`where` `terminal`), only `notify` or `tellMe`: the app can't see a terminal run end.
- `onlyIf` is never on the first step. `passed` and `stillFails` need a `waitForChecks` before it; `pushed` and `notPushed` need an `agent` step before it that may push; `approve` and `requestChanges` need an `agent` step before it in the background whose preset is `readOnly`.
- `waitForChecks`, `merge`, and `postReview` act on pull requests alone, so the routine's watch must hold pull requests.
- A schedule's `digest` needs `notify` as the first step.
